```python
for attempt in range(3):
    try:
        return requests.post("/orders", json=order, timeout=2)
    except Timeout:
        continue   # the first attempt may have already succeeded server-side
```
The request can time out on the *client* side after the server already processed it — the order was created, but the response never made it back before the timeout fired. This loop then submits the same order again, creating a duplicate, unless the endpoint itself is idempotent. See [idempotency and exactly-once](idempotency-and-exactly-once) for the fix.
